FYDE: v3d minigbm: allocate shared/scanout buffers from CMA dma-heap (Option A)

Prototype of the "Option A" design: replace the kernel-side vc4 buffer borrow
(VC4_CALLOC ioctl flag -> v3d_vc4_bind.c, plus the vc4_drm / vc4_drm_file
globals) with a standard dma-buf flow.

For scanout / SW / video (V3D_HEAP_USE) buffers, the v3d minigbm backend now:
  1. allocates a contiguous, linear buffer from the CMA dma-buf heap
     (/dev/dma_heap/<cma-area>), and
  2. imports it into v3d via PRIME (drmPrimeFDToHandle). v3d maps the CMA
     pages into the GPU MMU through its existing gem_prime_import_sg_table ->
     v3d_bo_create_finish path, so the GPU can render into it. The same
     dma-buf is what vc4 imports for HVS scanout.

Pure-GPU (non-shared) buffers keep their native UIF-tiled v3d allocation.
CPU map / export / sync go through the dma-buf fd (mmap + DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC).

If no CMA heap is present at runtime the backend falls back to the legacy
VC4_CALLOC borrow, so behaviour never regresses. This removes the need for the
in-kernel v3d<->vc4 coupling once validated on-device.

Requires kernel: CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS_CMA=y (already set) and a CMA region
large enough for framebuffers (config.txt cma=... / reserved-memory).

BUG=none
TEST=emerge-rpi5-openfyde media-libs/minigbm
